Studio's sheets are Univer (https://github.com/dream-num/univer, Apache License 2.0, Copyright
DreamNum Co., Ltd.), reading and writing Excel files with ExcelJS (https://github.com/exceljs/exceljs,
MIT). Its documents are edited with Tiptap (https://github.com/ueberdosis/tiptap, MIT) on ProseMirror
(MIT), Markdown through tiptap-markdown (MIT), Word files read with mammoth
(https://github.com/mwilliamson/mammoth.js, BSD 2-Clause) and written with docx
(https://github.com/dolanmiu/docx, MIT), their look read with JSZip (https://github.com/Stuk/jszip,
MIT, offered as MIT or GPLv3; used under MIT). Decks are drawn with Marp Core (https://github.com/marp-team/marp-core,
MIT) and saved as PowerPoint with PptxGenJS (https://github.com/gitbrent/PptxGenJS, MIT). Studio is
built on the same open parts as GenOffice (https://github.com/genspark-ai/genoffice, Apache License 2.0);
it isn't affiliated with Genspark or GenOffice, whose names belong to Mainfunc, Inc.

Whiteboards are drawn with Excalidraw (https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw, MIT License), which
turns Mermaid into shapes with mermaid-to-excalidraw (MIT) and Mermaid
(https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid, MIT). Excalidraw's fonts are included as it ships them:
Excalifont, Virgil, Nunito, Lilita One, Assistant, Cascadia Code and Liberation Sans under the SIL
Open Font License 1.1, and Comic Shanns under the MIT License. Diagrams are laid out with dagre
(https://github.com/dagrejs/dagre, MIT, in the dagre-d3-es form Mermaid uses, MIT, with lodash-es,
MIT) and saved as PDF with jsPDF (https://github.com/parallax/jsPDF, MIT) and svg2pdf.js
(https://github.com/yWorks/svg2pdf.js, MIT), with what they use: fflate, fast-png, iobuffer, cssesc,
svgpath, specificity, font-family-papandreou and @babel/runtime (MIT) and pako (MIT and zlib
licenses). The whiteboard's shape icons are from Tabler Icons (https://tabler.io/icons, MIT License,
Copyright (c) 2020-2026 Paweł Kuna); the AWS, Azure and Google Cloud sets draw those same icons in
each cloud's colours. AWS's, Microsoft's and Google's own architecture icons are not included: each
is offered by its owner for drawing diagrams and documents, not for passing on inside other software
(Microsoft's terms say so outright). AWS, Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud are
trademarks of their owners, named only to say which services the shapes stand for.

Speech to text on your computer runs on sherpa-onnx (https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx,
Apache License 2.0), installed with the app through npm. The speech models are not included: each
is downloaded from Hugging Face when you ask for it, under its own license. Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3
is by NVIDIA, under CC-BY-4.0 (https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3); Parakeet
unified EN 0.6B is by NVIDIA, under the NVIDIA Open Model License
(https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-unified-en-0.6b); both in the ONNX form made by the
sherpa-onnx project. The Whisper models are by OpenAI, under the MIT License, in whisper.cpp's
form. Telling voices apart in a room uses ERes2Net, by Alibaba's 3D-Speaker project, under the
Apache License 2.0 (https://www.modelscope.cn/models/iic/speech_eres2net_sv_en_voxceleb_16k), in the
ONNX form made by the sherpa-onnx project, downloaded when you first ask for it.

The local model runs on llama.cpp's llama-server (https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp, MIT License,
Copyright (c) 2023-2026 The ggml authors), whose release builds come with the desktop app, with
their licences beside them (and LLVM's OpenMP runtime on Windows, under its own licence, included
there). The model is not included: Gemma 4 E2B, by Google, under the Apache License 2.0
(https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E2B-it), in the GGUF form made by the llama.cpp project
(https://huggingface.co/ggml-org/gemma-4-E2B-it-GGUF), is downloaded from Hugging Face when you ask for it.
